==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|ang|heafoc}}, {{l|ang|heafuc}} (''Mercian''), {{l|ang|hafuc}}

===Etymology===
From an early form ''haebuc'', from {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*habukaz}}, possibly from a {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*kobuǵo}}, perhaps ultimately derived from {{m|ine-pro|*keh₂p-||seize}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{m|ofs|havek}} (West Frisian {{m|fy|hauk}}), Old Saxon {{m|osx|havuk}} (Low German {{m|nds|Haavk}}), Dutch {{m|nl|havik}}, Old High German {{m|goh|habuch}}, {{m|goh|habuh}} (German {{m|de|Habicht}}), Old Norse {{m|non|haukr}} (Swedish {{m|sv|hök}}, Danish {{m|da|høg}}, Norwegian {{m|no|hauk}}, Icelandic {{m|is|haukur}}). The word is related to a Slavic word for a bird of prey (Russian {{l|ru|кобец}}, Polish {{m|pl|kobuz}}). Compare also Latin {{m|la|capys}}, {{l|la|capus||bird of prey}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhɑvok/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
{{ang-noun|m|hafocas}}

# a [[hawk]]

